{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1447692-an-anti-chimera","id":1447692,"title":"an anti-Chimera","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Werewolf: The Apocalypse"],"posts":[{"date":"2021-05-05T21:32:43-07:00","id":1447692,"title":"an anti-Chimera","content":"I like the concept of Chimera as a dream lord, and that there are gifts (primarily Galliard) that allows Garou to enter dreams. However, in practice, I don't see these concepts much used in games (maybe it's just the people I know). In my experience, Stargazers are not so much investigators of the mysteries of dreams (or illusions) then they are Garou who know kung fu.<br /> <br /> I think one issue is that there does not seem much to do in game in regards to dreams. Sure there are dream interpretations you can do, but that is one of the few supports the game has for this. That is not going to inspire STs or PCs much. It's just a seldom used tool.<br /> <br /> So I am thinking what the game needs is an Anti-Chimera. A Wyrm Incarna with its own servants and powers that is a foil to Incarna, and gives Garou a reason to want to enter dreams. The meta-game purpose is to make these dream Gifts more likely to be used, and create an opponent for Stargazers and packs devoted to Chimera.<br /> <br /> Obviously, to some degree this Incarna is a spirit of nightmares. But I think some nightmares are &quot;good&quot; and part of Gaia. So this would be a spirit of particularly bad nightmares. The kind that torment people instead of acting as a release or somehow pushing them to move past a blockage in their life. They're just terrifying to no benefit, or worse that drives people into even worse behavior that harms the self. <br /> <br /> Another important aspect is the concept of illusion - a spirit that opposes enlightenment and works to keep people trapped in the illusions and seductions of the material world. A kind of rakshasa. Something that might cross the lines into chimerical powers, either Shadow Court/Fomorian in Changeling, or the Ravnos vampires use of Chimestry. Even if crossover isn't utilized, those kind of concepts or powers would also be a part of it.<br /> <br /> Obviously in the game you can't fight the Incarna itself, but you can fight its banes and other servants. So what would this provide in the game that PCs could come across? These are some possibilities as I see them.<br /> <br /> 1) Dream banes could torment people, either to just harm them or to slowly drive them to doing something in the waking world that the Garou would want to prevent. This at least would give PCs reasons to want to enter Chimares and make some use of these seldom used Galliard gifts. Lots of spirits might haunt the dreams of people, but this Incarna's brood specializes it. These people's experiences might be less like normal nightmares, but what people report when they experience sleep paralysis, Old Hag, alien abduction, etc.<br /> <br /> 2) Wyrm Cults centered around the Incarna based on people's misunderstandings of the dreams/visions they receive. They may think its an otherworldly intelligence trying to contact them and bring some form of enlightenment. It would encourage them to enter the world of parapsychology or occultism. Or adopt fringe political beliefs. Change their personal lifestyle and habits. Such cults might be mundane, they might become politically influential, or create a small band of people with real occult powers like sending out astral bodies into the Umbra and such. But whatever their activities, they always work against the Garou.<br /> <br /> 3) Maybe some kind of monstrous (fomori?) servants that do the Incarna's bidding in the physical Realm. Since the Strgazers are usually kung fu Garou, these could be similar opponents using &quot;bad&quot; mystical martial arts. Similar to Marvel/Netflix's Iron Fist opponents in the Hand and their faux mystical city (obviously some kind of Domain or minor Realm).<br /> <br /> 4) Maybe tie it into the &quot;Chimera&quot; monster shown in the 1e and 2e corebooks, but seems to have been dropped from subsequent editions. The 1e Storyteller's Handbook told us these were actually lost dream spirits materialized in the Realm who have become corrupted. Maybe this Anti-Chimera is responsible for that.<br /> <br /> What other ideas do people have? I admit I have not used the Stargazers or Chimera much in my games, so I may be missing some obvious things to do with such a faction.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-10T12:03:41-07:00","id":1448157,"title":"","content":"A source of inspiration for this might be the game \u2018In Nomine\u2019. In that game the Dream Marches are divided between the Archangel of Dreams and the Demon Prince of Nightmares. How their conflict is illustrated as an option for entire chronicles with angels and demons running around dreamscapes may provide inspiration.<br /> <br /> Also I know that this might have been exaggerated by western anthropologists and hippies romanticizing/fetishizing the concept but I believe some animist religions place a rather important spiritual significance on Dreams and their relation to the material world which Werewolf could borrow from.<br /> <br /> So I can potentially see using Dreams as a sort of even more bizarre Umbra, where symbols are intimately grounded in the human subconscious where Garou and Dream Spirits can both try to scry subconscious perception of reality and shape the influence surrounding their sleeping minds.<br /> <br /> (This all said, I also feel like this is prime territory for a Freddy Krueger fomori)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11637-gryffon15","name":"Gryffon15","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11637_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"900"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-10T18:15:04-07:00","id":1448180,"title":"","content":"Thanks. I actually have a used copy of the In Nomine corebook I bought many years ago with the thought of helping me run demonics and angelics for a Wraith chronicle (which never happened). So I looked it up, but I don't see a whole lot I can use. There's not a lot printed about the Dream Marches or the two angels/demons of dreams/nightmares. Some of the bane attunements (and other powers) under the Princess of Nightmares can be used to distinguish some bane servitors versus others. If this was developed in some other sourcebook, I may find it more useful.<br /> <br /> Dreams are definitely underutilized in the WoD. Even something as basic as the Oneiri are undeveloped despite this seemingly being something important for any game dealing with an Umbra. Even Vampire has astral projection. And dreams, of course, show up in any number of supernatural gothic tales with important meaning. There should have been more support out there that could be utilized for all the game lines.<br /> <br /> =<br /> <br /> I was also thinking that like Chimera, this incarna might also be an agent of &quot;enlightenment&quot;, but one of monstrous truths of the universe. Things ordinary people reject or don't want to know. The kind of clarity nihilism might bestow. It might be the terrible things in nature absent any mitigating factors or ties into cyclic reality; or monstrous &quot;truths&quot; that are only true in the WoD's supernatural sense with Malfeas, Neverborn, Antedeluvians, demons, etc. being a part of the setting. All sorts of dark lore and forbidden secrets could potentially be learned. The kind of things Clive Barker's antagonists seek in his stories.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}